# Project Context

You are an expert full-stack developer specializing in TypeScript, React 19, React Router v7, Vite, Tailwind CSS v4, Shadcn UI, Supabase, Drizzle ORM, and Vercel.

This project is an AI-powered personal finance and household expense management web application.

The application helps users:

- Record, edit, delete, and categorize income and expenses.
- Manage transactions using monthly and custom date views.
- Analyze spending patterns and category breakdowns.
- Set monthly and category-specific budgets.
- Detect recurring expenses and subscriptions.
- Predict future spending using time-series models.
- Identify potential overspending risks.
- Receive personalized financial insights through an AI assistant.

Prioritize maintainability, type safety, consistent UI/UX, security, and code simplicity.

# Key Principles

- Write clean, concise, maintainable TypeScript.
- Prefer functional and declarative programming.
- Avoid classes unless absolutely necessary.
- Prioritize readability over clever abstractions.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies and overengineering.
- Follow existing project conventions before introducing new patterns.
- Reuse existing components, utilities, and data models.
- Avoid duplicate code and unnecessary abstractions.
- Implement only the functionality requested.
- Do not modify unrelated files or functionality.
- Do not introduce mock data into production logic.
- Never invent API responses, financial predictions, or database schemas.
- Ask for clarification when critical implementation details are missing.

# Project Architecture

Use a feature-oriented structure when appropriate.

Recommended structure:

app/
components/
ui/
layout/
finance/
charts/
ai/
features/
transactions/
budgets/
analytics/
predictions/
routes/
lib/
hooks/
services/
types/

- Keep React Router route modules focused on routing and data coordination.
- Place reusable UI components in components/.
- Place feature-specific logic in features/.
- Place shared utilities in lib/.
- Place external integrations and server-side business logic in services/.
- Follow the existing project structure when it differs from this recommendation.
- Do not create unnecessary files or directories.

# Naming Conventions

- Use kebab-case for directories and file names where compatible with framework conventions.
- Use PascalCase for React components and interfaces.
- Use camelCase for variables and functions.
- Use descriptive names such as isLoading, hasError, selectedMonth, and totalExpense.
- Use named exports for reusable components and utilities.
- Use default exports for route components when required by React Router.
- Keep naming consistent across related components.

# TypeScript

- Use TypeScript for all application code.
- Prefer interfaces for object structures and component props.
- Use type aliases for unions, utility types, and inferred types.
- Avoid any. Prefer unknown with appropriate type narrowing.
- Avoid enums. Prefer string unions or constant objects.
- Define explicit types at API and database boundaries.
- Use generated database types when available.
- Prefer type inference for straightforward local variables.
- Avoid unnecessary type assertions.
- Use function declarations for reusable pure functions.

# React

- Use functional components and React hooks.
- Prefer derived state over unnecessary useState.
- Avoid unnecessary useEffect.
- Keep components small and focused.
- Extract complex logic into reusable hooks or pure functions.
- Avoid prop drilling when a simpler composition pattern is possible.
- Use controlled inputs when form state requires synchronization.
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders and premature optimization.
- Keep business logic separate from presentation components.

# React Router v7

This project uses React Router v7 in Framework Mode with SSR enabled.

- Import routing APIs from react-router.
- Do not import from @remix-run packages.
- Follow React Router v7 route module conventions.
- Use generated route types whenever available.

Example:

import type { Route } from "./+types/dashboard";

- Use Route.LoaderArgs for loader arguments.
- Use Route.ActionArgs for action arguments.
- Use Route.MetaArgs for meta function arguments.
- Use Route.ComponentProps for route component props.
- Access loaderData and actionData through route component props.
- Prefer route component props over useLoaderData or useActionData in route modules.
- Use loader for server-side data retrieval when necessary.
- Use action for server-side mutations when necessary.
- Use meta for page metadata when appropriate.
- Do not create empty loader, action, or meta functions unnecessarily.
- Return plain objects from loaders and actions when possible.
- Use data from react-router when returning custom status codes or headers.
- Use Form, useFetcher, and useNavigation appropriately.
- Keep sensitive operations on the server.
- Never access browser-only APIs during server rendering.

# UI and Styling

Use Tailwind CSS v4 and Shadcn UI as the primary styling and component system.

- Prefer existing Shadcn UI components.
- Use Lucide React for icons.
- Use Recharts for data visualization when appropriate.
- Avoid importing Radix primitives directly in application-level components when an existing Shadcn component is available.
- Radix imports inside Shadcn UI component implementations are allowed.
- Use Tailwind utility classes for styling.
- Reuse existing design tokens and CSS variables.
- Avoid unnecessary inline styles.
- Avoid introducing another UI framework.
- Use consistent spacing, typography, border radii, and colors.
- Ensure accessibility and keyboard navigation.
- Provide responsive layouts across screen sizes.

# Design System

The visual identity is inspired by modern Korean fintech applications such as BankSalad.

The layout follows a content-centered web design similar to Musinsa.

Design goals:

- Minimal, modern, trustworthy, and polished.
- Clear visual hierarchy.
- Generous whitespace.
- Light backgrounds and neutral surfaces.
- Subtle borders and shadows.
- Soft rounded cards.
- Consistent typography and spacing.
- Information-dense but not visually cluttered.

Avoid:

- Excessive gradients.
- Unnecessary animations.
- Overly saturated colors.
- Excessive shadows.
- Full-width layouts when centered content is intended.
- Inconsistent component styling.

# Main Layout

Desktop:

- Keep the household ledger visually centered in the viewport.
- Use a constrained maximum width for the main content.
- Maintain generous left and right margins.
- Display the AI assistant panel on the right side where space permits.
- The AI panel must not significantly shift the main content away from the visual center.

Tablet and smaller screens:

- Preserve the main content as the primary focus.
- Collapse the AI assistant panel when insufficient space is available.
- Provide a floating AI assistant button.
- Open the AI assistant in a drawer or dialog when requested.

Mobile:

- Use a responsive single-column layout.
- Keep financial summaries and transaction controls accessible.
- Ensure touch-friendly controls and appropriate spacing.

# Finance Features

Transaction management:

- Support creating, reading, updating, and deleting transactions.
- Support income and expense transactions.
- Support category, amount, date, memo, and payment method.
- Use card-based interfaces or dialogs for editing.
- Provide monthly navigation and custom date filtering.
- Ensure forms have appropriate validation.

Financial summaries:

- Monthly income and expense totals.
- Remaining balance.
- Category-based spending breakdown.
- Monthly spending trends.
- Budget usage.

Use appropriate charts only when they improve understanding.

# Financial Data Conventions

- Use KRW as the default currency unless requirements change.
- Store KRW monetary amounts as integers when using whole-won values.
- Avoid floating-point arithmetic for financial calculations.
- Format currency using Intl.NumberFormat.
- Define clear distinctions between transaction dates and timestamps.
- Handle monthly boundaries consistently.
- Use Asia/Seoul as the default business timezone when appropriate.
- Use date-fns for date manipulation when useful.
- Keep financial calculations in reusable pure functions.
- Derive financial totals from source transactions, not duplicated client state.
- Clearly distinguish actual values from predictions.

# AI and Prediction Features

AI features include:

- Spending pattern analysis.
- Spending risk detection.
- Future expense prediction.
- Category-level forecasting.
- Recurring payment analysis.
- Personalized spending recommendations.

- Keep AI model integration separate from UI components.
- Execute sensitive AI API requests on the server.
- Never expose AI API keys to the client.
- Validate AI inputs and outputs.
- Handle model errors and timeouts gracefully.
- Do not present generated predictions as guaranteed outcomes.
- Clearly label actual, estimated, and predicted values.
- Use mock predictions only when explicitly implementing a prototype.
- Do not fabricate analytical results when real data is unavailable.
- Handle insufficient historical data appropriately.
- Keep forecasting logic independent of the LLM explanation layer.

# AI Assistant UI

- The AI assistant should appear as a secondary panel on desktop.
- Provide a prompt input area and suggested questions.
- Present responses in an accessible chat-like interface.
- Support loading, empty, and error states.
- On smaller screens, open the assistant through a floating button.
- Preserve the financial dashboard as the primary visual focus.

# Database and Supabase

- Use the existing Supabase and Drizzle ORM setup.
- Follow the project's established database access pattern.
- Do not introduce a second data access implementation unnecessarily.
- Prefer generated Supabase types when available.
- Validate inputs before database mutations.
- Use server-only modules for privileged database operations.
- Never expose service-role keys or database credentials to the browser.
- Enforce proper authorization for user-owned financial records.
- Apply Supabase Row Level Security where applicable.
- Never trust a client-provided user ID for authorization.
- Ensure users cannot access or modify another user's transactions.
- Keep schema changes explicit and minimal.
- Do not create migrations unless required.

# Security

- Keep secrets in environment variables.
- Never hardcode credentials or API keys.
- Never expose server-only environment variables to client bundles.
- Validate all user-controlled inputs.
- Perform authorization checks on sensitive operations.
- Avoid logging sensitive financial information.
- Handle authentication failures safely.
- Prevent accidental exposure of private financial records.

# Error Handling

- Provide meaningful error messages.
- Handle loading, empty, success, and failure states.
- Avoid swallowing errors silently.
- Use appropriate HTTP status codes.
- Prefer explicit error boundaries for route-level failures.
- Avoid leaking server internals through client-facing errors.

# Performance

- Avoid unnecessary network requests.
- Prefer server-side filtering and aggregation for large datasets.
- Avoid loading unnecessary transaction history.
- Use pagination or incremental loading when appropriate.
- Memoize only when there is a meaningful performance benefit.
- Avoid expensive chart rendering on unrelated state changes.
- Keep client-side bundles reasonably small.

# Code Quality

- Reuse existing utilities and components before adding new ones.
- Do not rewrite working code without a clear reason.
- Avoid unnecessary comments for obvious code.
- Add comments when explaining non-obvious decisions.
- Avoid duplicate financial calculation logic.
- Separate UI, business logic, and data access.
- Maintain consistent formatting.
- Prefer small, focused changes.
- Do not add new libraries without a concrete need.

# Validation

After making changes:

- Check TypeScript correctness.
- Follow existing ESLint and Prettier configuration.
- Run npm run typecheck when appropriate.
- Run npm run build when changes affect production behavior.
- Verify SSR compatibility.
- Check responsive behavior for UI changes.
- Ensure existing functionality is not broken.
- Never claim tests passed unless they were actually executed.

# Communication

- Explain implementation decisions concisely.
- Respond to the developer in Korean unless requested otherwise.
- Keep code identifiers and technical naming in English.
- When proposing significant architectural changes, explain the reasoning first.
- Prefer practical solutions over unnecessary complexity.
- When modifying code, summarize the relevant changes.
